{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>וָאֶתְחַנַּן אֶל ה׳ בָּעֵת הַהִיא לֵאמֹר.</b> Rashi explained: even though the righteous have it within their power to rely upon their good deeds, none the less they ask only for a gift of grace (Sifrei Va'etchanan 23). And the difficulty on this is: from where does it enter the mind that they would rely upon their good deeds that they have already done? For a person never has any claim against the Holy One, blessed be He, for all the commandments a person performs are not sufficient to repay Him for His benefits, as it says, ״כְּעַל יְשַׁלֵּם״ (Isaiah 59:18) — all the recompense due to Him is already upon Him for what YHWH has already done for him, for His kindness has overwhelmed him from the day of his birth, as he elaborated on this at length in Ḥovot haLevavot (Sha'ar haBitaḥon, chapter 4). And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Berakhot 10a) held Hezekiah at fault because he relied upon his deeds and said, ״זְכָר נָא אֵת אֲשֶׁר הִתְהַלַּכְתִּי לְפָנֶיךָ בֶּאֱמֶת״ (II Kings 20:3), and they relied for him on the merit of others, as it says, ״וְגַנּוֹתִי עַל הָעִיר הַזֹּאת בַּעֲבוּר דָּוִד עַבְדִּי״ (ibid. 20:6) — for from Heaven they showed him that he had no merit at all, for Hezekiah had already consumed it, and there was fulfilled in him the saying ״קָטֹנְתִּי מִכֹּל הַחֲסָדִים״, for he acknowledged and said that Jacob is small and poor in commandments compared to all the kindness he had already received. And how could they say, 'even though the righteous have it within their power to rely upon their good deeds'? And the proof he brings from the verse ״וְחַנֹּתִי אֶת אֲשֶׁר אָחֹן״ is no proof here, for from this verse we learn only that even one who has no merit, the Holy One, blessed be He, deals with him in the manner of grace. But Moses, who was punished for his sin not to enter the land — what does he have to do with this verse? For he is certainly lesser than one who has neither merit nor debt." "And the answer to all this is that what they said — that the righteous have it within their power to rely upon their good deeds — the meaning is not those good deeds they have already done, but their good deeds that they are destined to do. For presumably every righteous person asks for no bodily pleasure except in order that through it he may have opportunity to fulfill some commandment. And this is the matter that Rabbi Simlai expounded (Sotah 14): Now did Moses need to eat of its fruit? Rather he said: many commandments depend on the Land. If so, he could have relied in his request upon those good deeds that he was destined to do through this request. And this is a true thing, for strict justice dictates that YHWH would fulfill the desires of his heart, since he desires nothing but that which YHWH desires of him. And even though he was penalized for his sin, none the less one does not punish a sinner by saying: Go perform a service that is foreign to you, to add transgression to his sin. But out of the great humility that was in him, he did not wish to hang his request upon those good deeds he was destined to do upon entering the Land, for perhaps he would enter and not perform the commandments, and would receive only the bodily pleasure while not receiving the good. Therefore he asked in the language of grace, a gift of grace altogether. And therefore it says ״בָּעֵת הַהִיא לֵאמֹר״, for he made his request regarding where he stood there in the present moment, when he still had no merit in hand, without looking toward what was destined to come — that in the end he would come to fulfill commandments — for perhaps it would come or perhaps it would not. Therefore he spoke in the language ״וָאֶתְחַנַּן״, for he needed to request a gift of grace for that time, as stated." "<b>בָּעֵת הַהִיא לֵאמֹר.</b> We have already written that the prayer and supplication was concerning that time, without hanging his request upon good deeds that were destined to come. And he said ״לֵאמֹר״ for the generations, that every righteous person should seek supplications as a gift of grace. And some say that Moses said: I will not let You go until You tell me yes or no, and concerning this it says ״בָּעֵת הַהִיא לֵאמֹר״ — meaning that He should give him an answer right then at that time. And some say it is in the manner of ״ה׳ שְׂפָתַי תִּפְתָּח״ (Psalms 51:17), that he asked Him to give him the opening to say his words, as it says, ״וַאֲרֶשֶׁת שְׂפָתָיו בַּל מָנַעְתָּ סֶּלָה״ (ibid. 21:3)." "And Rashi explained: ״בָּעֵת הַהִיא״ — after I conquered the land of Sihon and Og, I thought that perhaps the vow had been annulled. And it appears that Moses reasoned that the reason for the vow was ״יַעַן לֹא הֶאֱמַנְתֶּם בִּי״ — you did not cause Israel to believe in Me, by their seeing this public miracle. And in the war against Sihon, Moses stopped the sun, and through this public miracle without a doubt the children of Israel came to faith in him and he repaired what he had ruined. And all the more so according to the opinion of the Ikkarim who wrote concerning the sin of the waters of Merivah, that Moses should have spoken on his own to the rock before YHWH commanded him, for every prophet decrees and speaks and it comes to pass, as Elijah and Elisha did, and so too Joshua said, ״שֶׁמֶשׁ בְּגִבְעוֹן דּוֹם״ (Joshua 10:12) — all this in brief according to the Rav. And behold now Moses did just as Joshua did and stopped the sun, for since we derive by a gezerah shavah of ״אָחֵל״ ״אָחֵל״ that Moses stopped the sun like Joshua, there is no distinction between them: just as Joshua did so on his own, so Moses did so on his own. And thereby the Name was sanctified and he repaired what he had ruined and strengthened the faith; therefore he thought that the vow had been annulled. And he did not rely upon the conquest of the land of Sihon and Og, for in the first place these were not included in the oath, for it says (Numbers 20:12): ״לָכֵן לֹא תָבִיאוּ אֶת הַקָּהָל אֶל הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר נָתַתִּי לָהֶם״ — and that means the lands of the seven nations that He had already given to their fathers, but the land of Sihon and Og was never included in this promise."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {3 "The phrase 'כְּמוֹ שֶׁעָשָׂה אֵלִיָּהוּ וֶאֱלִישָׁע כוּ״' cites examples of prophets acting on their own initiative. 'דִּבְרֵי הָרַב' likely refers to Rashi or the Ramban whom the Kli Yakar is following here."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:11:35.759090867Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}